# The supply of Bratislava new-builds keeps growing, prices are standing still

{cqNameEn} of {cqYear} on the Bratislava new-build market means stronger demand, price stagnation and a further increase in the number of flats for sale. Sales reached {cqSales} flats against {pqSales} in {pqLabel}. The price level was unchanged and rose by {yqM2PctEn}% year on year. The supply of flats for sale rose to {cqSupply}.

As of {asOfEn} the supply on the new-build market consisted, according to Residata's data, of {cqSupply} flats across {cqProjects} projects. Against {pqLabel} supply rose by {pqSupply} flats or {pqSupplyPctEn}%, while year on year it rose by {yqSupply} flats or {yqSupplyPctEn}%.

The average price of these flats on offer reached {cqM2} €/m² incl. VAT, which year on year is an increase of only {yqM2PctEn}%. A year ago the average price was {yqM2} €/m² incl. VAT and a quarter ago {pqM2} €/m² incl. VAT.

A standing average does not mean developers leave prices alone. Over the quarter the price of {rpUnits} Bratislava flats changed across {rpProjects} projects, {rpChanges} times in all; {rpUpEn}% of the changes were increases and the median change was {rpStepEn}%. It touched only {rpShareEn}% of the tracked offer, however, so across the offer as a whole it shifts the average by roughly {rpEffectEn}%.

Working against it is the composition of supply. Over the same period {qNewUnits} flats came onto the market at an average of {qNewMeanM2} €/m² incl. VAT, that is {newBelowPctEn}% below the market level. Existing flats getting dearer and newly listed ones arriving cheaper very nearly cancel out — and an average that does not move is the result of those two opposing forces, not of a market standing still.

The average absolute price of flats on offer stood at {cqPrice} € incl. VAT, while their average floor area reached {cqAreaEn} m².

